According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are different for function $f$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}f(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}f(x)=4}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}f(x)=3}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}g(x)=\lim_{x\to 1^+}g(x)=1}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}$ using the fact that the limit of a quotient of functions is the quotient of the individual limits (as long as the limit in the denominator isn't equal to $0$):

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}f(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}g(x)}
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}f(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}g(x)}
\end{align}$

Let's find the limit from the left:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}f(x)}}{\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}g(x)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD 4}{\maroonD 1}
\\\\
&=4
\end{align}$

Now let's find the limit from the right:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\purpleC{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}f(x)}}{\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}g(x)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\purpleC 3}{\maroonD 1}
\\\\
&=3
\end{align}$

The one-sided limits are different and therefore the limit doesn't exist.
